\begin_body
\begin_layout Title
The Economics of Superstars: Music Sales and Concerts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Victor H. Aguiar
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
ECON 803, Fall 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Problem 10.4 [Applied]. Questions only. Part I uses consumer theory from Chapter 1; Parts II through IV combine cost minimization and monopoly pricing. This handout uses the same questions as the textbook.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taylor S. is a stylized superstar whose fans consume music services and concerts. This is the graduate version of the music-and-concerts case, with explicit preferences, technology, and market assumptions. All numbers are hypothetical; the model is not an empirical description of Taylor Swift's contracts. Quantities are divisible, and the mass of identical fans is one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Part I. Fan demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Write \begin_inset Formula $q_M,q_C$\end_inset
 for music and concert services, the two coordinates of consumption. A fan has wealth \begin_inset Formula $w>0$\end_inset
, prices \begin_inset Formula $p_M,p_C>0$\end_inset
, and utility \begin_inset Formula $u(q_M,q_C)=(\tfrac12 q_M^\rho+\tfrac12 q_C^\rho)^{1/\rho}$\end_inset
, where \begin_inset Formula $0<\rho<1$\end_inset
. The letters \begin_inset Formula $M,C$\end_inset
 are local commodity labels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Formulate the budget problem and derive demands using \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1/(1-\rho)$\end_inset
. For the remaining parts set \begin_inset Formula $\rho=1/2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $w=1$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $p_M=1$\end_inset
. Explain why music demand changes when the concert price changes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Part II. Production
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For \begin_inset Formula $j\in\{M,C\}$\end_inset
 the artist's technology is \begin_inset Formula $y_j=A_j\sqrt{n_j}$\end_inset
, where \begin_inset Formula $n_j\ge0$\end_inset
 is labor and \begin_inset Formula $A_j>0$\end_inset
 productivity. Labor costs \begin_inset Formula $\nu>0$\end_inset
 per hour, distinct from fan wealth \begin_inset Formula $w$\end_inset
. The processes are independent. This stylized music-service cost does not describe the cost of creating an original recording as a function of its streams.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Derive \begin_inset Formula $C_j(\nu,y_j)$\end_inset
, average cost, and marginal cost. Explain decreasing returns. Set \begin_inset Formula $A_M=A_C=\nu=1$\end_inset
 in the numerical parts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Part III. A share of volume and receipts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An exogenous \begin_inset Formula $\tau\in(0,1]$\end_inset
 is the fraction of music consumption attributable to this artist. Her output is \begin_inset Formula $y_M=\tau q_M$\end_inset
 and her music receipts are \begin_inset Formula $\tau p_Mq_M$\end_inset
. Concert output is \begin_inset Formula $y_C=q_C$\end_inset
. She takes \begin_inset Formula $p_M,\tau$\end_inset
 as given and chooses \begin_inset Formula $p_C>0$\end_inset
. There is no shared labor constraint or fixed cost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) Write total revenue and profit after substituting demand and costs. Distinguish a revenue derivative with respect to ticket price from marginal revenue with respect to output. Account for changes in both revenue streams and production costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(d) Derive the first-order condition for the optimal concert price. Examine profit as \begin_inset Formula $p_C\downarrow0$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $p_C\to\infty$\end_inset
. Establish global optimality and uniqueness, analytically or with a justified numerical method accounting for every stationary point and boundary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(e) Compare \begin_inset Formula $\tau=1$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $\tau=1/2$\end_inset
. In a table report optimal concert prices, both fan quantities, both delivered outputs, revenues, costs, and profits. State numerical accuracy. Does the concert price rise or fall when the music share falls?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(f) Use the first-order condition or implicit differentiation to analyze the price response for \begin_inset Formula $\tau\in[1/2,1]$\end_inset
. Interpret the roles of substitution and production costs. Do not assume the same sign for every technology or parameter value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Part IV. A royalty share
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now \begin_inset Formula $\tau$\end_inset
 changes only the fraction of music revenue paid to the artist: music output is \begin_inset Formula $y_M=q_M$\end_inset
, while receipts remain \begin_inset Formula $\tau p_Mq_M$\end_inset
. All other assumptions are unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(g) Write the revised profit function and repeat the comparison between \begin_inset Formula $\tau=1$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $\tau=1/2$\end_inset
. Identify the changed cost channel and determine whether the earlier price response survives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(h) Explain what evidence distinguishes a fall in music volume from a fall in the royalty fraction. Discuss the roles of CES substitution, decreasing returns, and control of concert prices. Contrast the artist's decision with price-taking supply in the main chapter.
\end_layout

